dsv41 patch 0084 (23.–25.10.2026, v26h): KV offload, load a prefix larger than the per-request cap (0081) in rounds.
Why: 0081 caps a load at 66,432 tokens (0.45 of the 256 MiB CPU tier) so that a large prefix cannot hang the tiering
manager; everything past the cap was computed again. A 950K-token prefix after a restart therefore cost a full prefill
(440 s at priority 0, 770 s at background priority with 0074) although all of it sat in the file tier.
How: when a lookup was cut by the default cap and more of the prompt remains, the request keeps its blocks and its exact
boundary after the load (no replay yet) and is looked up again from there in the next scheduling pass, one round of up
to 66,432 tokens at a time, until a round comes back short; then the usual SWA-bounded replay runs. Each follow-up
round is admitted by a side-effect-free check that mirrors allocate_slots after the round's preparation (blocks below
the sliding window released, block tables padded with null blocks, as a first-time allocation lays them out) with the
full-ISL gate kept on; a refused round keeps everything and waits (logged once per boundary, warned every 60 s). A
round reserves only for running/loading prefills and block holders ahead of it, so the oldest holder can always finish
(two reloads cannot wait for each other); a refused holder keeps new requests of the same or lower priority out of
that pass, more urgent ones still go ahead. A follow-up lookup with 0 hits after a full round means the CPU tier is
busy with other loads: retried for up to VLLM_DSV41_ROUND_ZERO_WAIT_S (30 s) before the rest is computed (v26f).
Admission control (v26h): at most N requests (default floor(1 / load fraction) = 2,
VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_MAX_LOADS) may have offload work in flight. Another request whose next chunk is offloaded waits
without starting any promotion (the tiering manager is asked only whether the first chunk the CPU tier lacks sits
in the file tier) for at most VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_ADMIT_WAIT_S (120 s, absolute), priority first; a fresh prompt never
waits. Without it a third simultaneous reload got MISS ("stored, but CPU tier full") and was computed; retrying
inside the manager instead livelocked three reloads.
Rounds continue only past the 0081 default cap, never past a max_load_tokens the client set.
Measured (24.10., 2× GB10, 1M context): 950K prefix after a restart, first token 11.2 s instead of 440 s, 300K 4.0 s;
KL of the first 8 tokens' top-20 against a cold computation (cache_salt) <= 0.00003 (cold vs cold 0.00025), top-1 8/8.
Three 300K reloads at once: all three from the offload in 5.3–7.0 s (KL <= 0.00012, top-1 8/8; the third waited
1.7 s for a slot), interactive first token 0.1 s, a fresh 20K prompt meanwhile 8.8 s (its normal prefill).
Switch: VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_ROUNDS=1 (default off = behaviour of 0081).
